      "user": "Why might I avoid hearing it now, even though I like the song?",
      "assistant": "The rain tasted of rust. It wasn't a heavy rain, not the kind that hammered the corrugated iron roofs of Havenwood. It was a slow, persistent bleed, staining the grey streets and clinging to the edges of everything. I hadn't noticed it before, not really. Just the way it coated the chipped porcelain of the memorial.\n\nThere was a single, perfect poppy blooming there, a defiant crimson against the perpetual dusk. I’d been tracing its petals with a fingertip, a gesture I’d repeated a thousand times, each one a little less deliberate.\n\nThe song started then. Not with a crash, not with a fanfare. Just a low, insistent hum, like the drone of a forgotten engine. It was… something. I can’t quite grasp it. A saxophone, maybe. Worn, thin, weeping. It was called \"The Cartographer's Daughter.\"\n\nI didn't turn it on. I didn't even *let* it reach me. I just… felt it. A pressure behind my eyes, a tightening in my chest that wasn't the rain. It wasn't a memory, not exactly. More like a fracture.\n\nI’ve been collecting things since. Small,",
